export const PREAMBLE =
  "# Motion matrix\n\nThe grid below is generated from `motion.jsonl` -- the source of record, which\ncarries the provenance the `*` convention only hints at. Edit the jsonl and\nregenerate; do not hand-edit this file.\n\nHOW each component can move, and what each movement consumes. One row per\n`(component, part, transition)`. A row declares mechanics and its complete consumption\nset -- duration tier, curve role, delays, extents -- every dimension a first-class\ncolumn, none ever implied. A row never carries a raw value. Values live in the system\ntokens (`--rafters-duration-fast` and kin); Studio says what `duration-fast` is; an\nintent preset is nothing but a value-set for the system tokens. ZERO IS A VALUE.\n\nRows are assigned by need, not by cross-product. A cell exists because the component's\nbehaviour has that moment; no cell exists for a moment the component does not have.\nComponents that need a speed choice declare a variant in their own source (CVA, like\n`size`) -- variants are in the component, not in this matrix, which records only the\ndefault assignment.\n\nAnnotation provenance: unmarked assignments carry the July efficient baseline\n(legion 019f956f). Assignments marked `*` are PROPOSED here and unreviewed -- treat\nthem as a starting position for the knobs, not as measured. Nothing in this file was\nderived; there is no formula upstream of these cells.\n\nThis file supersedes the token-vocabulary matrix (branch `docs/motion-matrix`,\ncca2d50e). The 13 semantic motion tokens are deleted by ruling (2026-08-02). The old\nfile's \"holes\" (thumb-travel, indicator-move, slide-advance, value-change, state-swap)\nare movements here, not holes.\n\n## Token generics -- five namespaces of equal rank\n\nEvery namespace gets its own `--rafters-*` vars and its own utilities (all generated by\nus -- `--duration-*` is not a Tailwind v4 theme namespace, nothing rides on theme\ninference). Studio writes the values.\n\n| namespace | members | notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `duration-*` | instant, micro, fast, moderate, normal, slow | banded ranges; band is perception, position is character |\n| `ease-*` | standard, enter, exit, linear, spring-smooth, spring-snappy | roles; curve assignment is explicit per cell, never inferred from a transition's name |\n| `delay-*` | hover-intent, linger, choreo-step, stagger-step, skip | first-class; replaces hardcoded 200ms in tooltip and navigation-menu. `skip` is the warm-reopen grace: moving between adjacent tooltips/hover targets while one was just open reopens without the intent delay |\n| `extent-*` | pop (zoom start scale), press (depression scale), draw (indicator draw) | the geometry of a movement; half the personality lives here -- friendly pops big, elegant whispers in |\n| `period-*` | spin, pulse, blink, shimmer | loops; intent-invariant by ruling but ride the same var mechanism so they remain reachable |\n\nSlide distances stay geometric (percent of own size, or spacing-derived), marked\n`structural` below -- as are fixed angles (a chevron's 180deg is mechanics, not tuning).\n\n## Movement vocabulary -- mechanics, not tokens\n\n| movement | properties | notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `fade` | opacity | |\n| `slide` | transform: translate | axis, and a side where anchored |\n| `zoom` | transform: scale | anchored popups scale from the anchor origin; consumes `extent-pop` |\n| `rotate` | transform: rotate | never combined with translate (combination constraint) |\n| `reveal` | grid-rows / height | disclosure on y |\n| `travel` | position along a track | distance IS the travel; see the pointer rule |\n| `fill` | inline-size / width | value displays |\n| `swap` | discrete state change | at zero it is an instant swap, a legitimate personality |\n| `color` | background, text, border | |\n| `elevation` | box-shadow | |\n| `ring` | outline / ring | focus |\n| `loop` | keyframes, infinite | takes a period, not a duration |\n| `delay` | time before/between movements | choreography |\n\n**The pointer rule (travel):** while a pointer drives the part, the part tracks the\npointer EXACTLY -- no animation, not a tunable cell, any nonzero value is a defect.\nThe same part moving by a discrete step (keyboard, index change, snap on release)\nanimates, and the step distance is the travel.\n\n**The anchor rule (reposition):** an open anchored popup tracking its anchor -- scroll,\nresize, collision flip or shift (`collision-detector`, `float`) -- repositions\nINSTANTLY. Never animated, not a tunable cell: an animated reposition reads as the\npopup wandering. Only presence (open/close) animates; position while open is tracking,\nsame family as the pointer rule.\n\n**Presence:** enter/exit cells assume keyframes on mount and behaviour-owned unmount --\nthe behaviour layer holds removal until `animationend` (`use-presence` is the\nmechanism). No `@starting-style` dependency; it is not trusted outside Tailwind's\npipeline.\n\n**Choreography:** parts sequence via `delay-choreo-step` between their rows. Collection\npopups (marked C) carry an items row with `delay-stagger-step`; zero (all items\ntogether) is the expected value for most intents.\n\n**The input rule:** layout change driven by the user's own input stream -- textarea\nautosize while typing, combobox list reflow per keystroke -- tracks instantly. Animating\nit puts lag between a person and their own hands. Third member of the tracking family\n(pointer rule, anchor rule).\n\n**Reduced motion is law, not a cell:** every cell in this matrix resolves to zero under\n`prefers-reduced-motion` except loops that communicate ongoing work (spinner, progress\nindeterminate), which may slow but not stop -- stillness there reads as frozen. This is\nsystem behaviour above every intent; no preset can override it.\n\n**Logical directions:** slide sides and travel axes are logical (inline-start/end), not\nphysical -- RTL flips them for free. A cell that names a physical side is a defect.\n\n**Out of scope for this matrix:** page-level transitions (framework/view-transition\nterritory -- the old `motion-page` token died with no consumer for exactly this reason);\nscroll-driven reveals (site-level composition, not component behaviour); and\ncross-component choreography (ruled out 2026-08-02: it requires global animation state\nand this stack has none -- neither does the platform underneath it).\n\n**Loops are intent-invariant** by standing ruling. Their cells exist here (the period\nis a setting) but sit outside the intent presets.\n\n## The grid\n\nColumns: duration (tier, or period for loops) | curve (role) | delay (generics\nconsumed) | extent. `--` means none. `*` means proposed, unreviewed.\n";

